Full pattern in hand. For the spike client.tsx:

```tsx
import type { Context } from '@deepseek-ai/cordis';
import type {} from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-documentpreview/client';
import type {} from '@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-right/client';
import { createPlateEditor, Plate, PlateContent } from 'platejs/react';

export const name = 'workdsh-plate-client';
export const inject = ['slots', 'documentPreviews'];

function PlateSpikeDocument() {
  const [editor] = useState(() => createPlateEditor({ plugins: [], value: [{type:'p', children:[{text:'Plate spike：在这里输入。'}]}] }));
  return (
    <div style={{height:'100%', overflow:'auto', padding:16}}>
      <Plate editor={editor}>
        <PlateContent placeholder="输入..." style={{outline:'none'}} />
      </Plate>
    </div>
  );
}

export function apply(ctx: Context): void {
  ctx.effect(() => ctx.documentPreviews.register({
    id: 'workdsh-plate',
    extensions: ['plate'],
    title: () => 'Plate 文档',
    priority: 'builtin',
    loading: 'bytes-complete',
  }));
  ctx.slots.inject('sidebar.right.tab.document', () =>
    ctx.slots.register(
      { name: 'sidebar.right.tab.document', key: 'workdsh-plate' },
      PlateSpikeDocument,
    ),
  );
}
```

Questions:
- Component signature: slots.register(name-options, Component) — OfficeDocument receives injected props. For spike, component ignores props. The slot 'sidebar.right.tab.document' — how does a tab get a label/icon? Check how the right sidebar renders tabs — the slots registration for tab.document probably shows registered documents when a document is open. Hmm — actually "sidebar.right.tab.document" is the right sidebar tab that appears in document context. The office one shows the office document editor. For our spike, registering shows our component in the same slot — but when does it appear? When a document preview is active. Since we don't yet produce .plate documents, the tab may never show. For spike visibility, maybe better to ALSO test a simpler always-visible mount? The design says mounts = sidebar.right.tab.document + documentPreviews .plate. To make the tab appear, we need a .plate document open — which needs document creation (M2). 

Alternative spike evidence for S5: the registration itself succeeds (no error) + the documentPreviews registry lists our provider. We can verify via probe in browser console: ctx not accessible... Hmm.

Simpler: create a sample .plate file in Library? Library imports material — a .plate file (JSON) imported to library, then preview it → documentPreviews picks our registered handler (extensions ['plate']) → our component renders. That exercises BOTH mounts at once (document preview + right tab in document context). But does library preview use documentPreviews registry? The office registration with extensions docx/xlsx/pptx powers library previews of those files, yes (loading: 'bytes-complete' = the preview gets bytes). But our component in the spike ignores bytes. OK — for spike, rendering anything on a .plate preview is enough.

Actually wait: documentPreviews.register — who consumes it? The document preview pane (sidebar.right document tab host). The flow: library file click → preview opens in right sidebar → documentPreviews matches extension → renders registered client in "document" tab context. So importing a test.plate into Library via UI then clicking it would show our editor. That's a real end-to-end S5.

The inject: for office, `inject: () => ({office})` passes the office client model. Spike: no inject needed? slots.register options type may require inject optional — office has it; connectors? Let me not overthink: omit inject, component self-contained.

package.json additions:
- exports './client': './dist/client.browser.js'
- dsh.client = { platform: 'web', inject: [...what...] } — office's dsh.client.inject lists plugin packages providing client services (@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-documentpreview etc.). For spike: ["@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-documentpreview", "@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-slots"?...] — office lists sidebar-right, renderer, etc. Minimal: documentpreview + sidebar-right + renderer? slots service comes from dsh-client-ui-slots probably. office's inject array: ["@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-documentpreview","@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-renderer","@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-sidebar-right", "@deepseek-ai/dsh-api-session-controller","@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-connection","@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-chat","@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-conversation","@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-input-trigger"]. For spike I need: sidebar-documentpreview (documentPreviews service), sidebar-right (the tab slot), and whatever provides 'slots' (dsh-client-ui-slots). Copy minimal three: documentpreview, sidebar-right, slots.

Build script: scripts/build-plate.mjs modeled on build-connectors.mjs (esbuild cjs, external react + react/jsx-runtime + primitives). platejs/react imports react — external OK. Does platejs import 'react-dom'? PlateContent might. Add react-dom externals if needed — host provides? office externalizes only react & react/jsx-runtime & primitives; react-dom is bundled in office's deps. For spike, bundle react-dom if platejs needs it... but two React copies (host react external + bundled react-dom) breaks hooks. Slate-react uses react-dom? Possibly for portals. Risk: moderate. Try external ['react','react/jsx-runtime','react-dom','react-dom/client'] first; if host doesn't provide react-dom to the module loader, bundle fails at runtime (require error) — then switch to bundling react-dom. Let me check how the ModuleLoader factory's require works — what modules are available to require? Presumably a fixed set (react, react/jsx-runtime, primitives). Check dsh-client-ui-renderer or the host's ModuleLoader implementation: grep window.__ModuleLoader__ in repo.

Let me check what the ModuleLoader require supports.

